On Mon, 16 Feb 2004 08:06:07 +0000, JohnWAlbright wrote:

> Problems I've had thus far are:

Solutions I've used to great effect:

> Food Poisoning

Don't ever eat zombie corpses.  In general, if you didn't see (n.b.
blinding, etc) it die, you don't know if it was a zombie or not.  If you
didn't see it die *recently* (last 50-100 turns), it may have rotted
enough to kill you regardless.

If you still manage to get food poisoning, #pray (early on) or (a)pply an
uncursed or preferably blessed unicorn horn.  Failing *that* (angry god,
etc), blessed potions of [extra,full] healing sometimes do it, too.

> Gnomish Mines (great place to get some good equipment but usually too
> tough)

Noodle around on Dlvl 1-4 until you're level 4 or so.  Then try.

I find the gnomes (gnome lords, gnome wizards, gnome king) and, depending
on race and alignment, the other denizens, to be a wonderful supplement to
my diet. They're plentiful and easy to kill.

> Wererats (lycanthropy stinks)

Ranged weapons.  Magic.  Thrown potions.  Wands.  Also, eating lycanthrope
corpses has the obvious consequences.  Unless you want that particular
kind of lycanthropy, DON'T DO IT.

Generally the "fighter" classes (Bar, Val, Sam, Cav, some others) can
*relatively* safely fight lesser weres (rats, jackals, similar) with melee
weapons -- low AC keeps them from biting, and high damage means they may
not live long enough to infect you.

> running out of food

Eat *everything* that isn't provably unsafe:
 - yellow molds will give you hallucinations
 - bats will frequently confuse or stun (I don't recall, because I
   stopped eating them)
 - zombie and were corpses, as above
 - acidic corpses (green mold, acid blob, etc) may kill you if you have
   low HP
 - cockatrice corpses and eggs should be obvious
 - green slimes will turn you into a green slime.  It's embarassing.
 - spare tripe rations are an emergency food -- depending on race, you
   *may* vomit and end up hungrier, because eating dog food is gross.
 - corpses over about 50-100 turns old may be rotten and give you food
   poisoning.

> generally running into monster that kick my butt and ruin all my fun.

Learn what the various monsters do.  There's really only one or two ways
to do that.  I opted for "die a lot".

Some pointers:
 - don't get into melee with a floating eye.  While *it* won't kill you,
   it'll paralyze you.  I have a number of entries in my score file that
   involve floating-eye paralysis and a really embarassing monster (little
   dog, dwarf).
 - keep a scroll of teleport around for emergencies, if possible.
 - DO eat a floating eye corpse as soon as possible.  They consistently
   give you ESP, which will let you tell where (minded) monsters on the
   level are when you're blind.  Blinding yourself safely is left as an
   excercise.
 - Early on, leave the fountains and sinks alone.  There's a number of
   unpleasant ways to die early on each:
    - fountains: Water demons, swarms of water moccasins, nymphs
    - sinks: Incubi/Succubi, black puddings, random potion effects

